A||N||2||merlin||Oil in Antifreeze, good compression and no smoke...  How?…||||11||
Z||000000||merlin||07-08-2001||07:26 PM||||It’s been an uphill battle with my 144,000 1987 V6 GT.  I’ve spent several months replacing valve cover gaskets, cat, timing chain components, and water pump.  Now, as I was flushing my coolant system after the new water pump install I noticed that each flush came out looking like muddy water.  Being afraid to admit there was a problem I continued flushing the coolant system (about 5 times) and each time it was the same story (muddy water).  I also noticed that the radiator cap had brown sludge around it, so it’s probably had oil leaking into the coolant for awhile before I replaced the water pump.<P>Anyway, I did a compression check less than 100 miles ago and the lowest cylinder compression was 145 lbs and the highest 163 lbs.  I would expect compression to be poor in one or two of those cylinders.  In addition, the car does not smoke once it is warmed up.  I guess what I’m boiling down to is this a blown head gasket?  It doesn’t seem to have the symptoms of a blown head gasket or am I just in denial? <BR>||||reg||11
Z||000001||rogergarrison||07-08-2001||07:30 PM||||i know of two people that had the same problem after replacing a water pump. seems there is supposed to be some special sealer applied to some of the mounting bolts in water pump. they go thru the water jackets/oil gallies. dont have any firsthand info, just what i was told.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Kerri 98||07-08-2001||08:50 PM||||Hate to tell ya Merlin, but it sounds like a blown head gasket and you're in denial.  Have you noticed an increase in how often you add oil? The smoke you speak of prior to its warm up....somewhat like what comes from a tail pipe when it's cold? Chocolatey appearance like a milkshake isn't a good thing. Have you removed the radiator cap and started the car up and seen it just explode out of the radiator? Lots of threads regarding ways to rule a head gasket out.  Try a search. Good Luck...Kerri||||reg||1
